---
/**
 * Brief behind the "Paying up for the boom" row on /economics/.
 * URL kept at /economics/still-1998-not-1999/ (judgment: avoid a 301; the
 * watch-list brief already linked here). Title is the row risk name. Backstory
 * is dated text. Where we stand and the levels in What we're watching are built
 * from the row's feeds at build time (src/lib/briefs.ts). The old digest is in
 * docs/archive/pages/economics/.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import RiskWatch from '../../components/RiskWatch.astro';
import { payingUpBriefNow } from '../../lib/briefs';
import '../../styles/economics.css';

const title = 'Paying up for the boom';
const subhead = 'This boom already has revenue and profit. The open question is the price paid for those earnings. The numbers below come from the /economics/ watch-list feed.';

const brief = payingUpBriefNow();

const watchItems = [
  ...brief.watch,
  'When private companies with thin wrappers, products built on someone else\'s model, try to raise at high multiples of revenue.',
  'Whether listed leaders keep reporting revenue and profit that can defend the price investors pay.',
];

const sources = [
  {
    text: 'Trueman, Wong, and Zhang, The Eyeballs Have It: Searching for the Value in Internet Stocks, Journal of Accounting Research, 2000',
    href: 'https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=206648',
  },
  {
    text: 'NVIDIA, financial results for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, 26 Aug 2026',
    href: 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027',
  },
  {
    text: 'Reuters, Anthropic revenue run rate tops $65 billion, 17 Aug 2026',
    href: 'https://www.reuters.com/technology/anthropic-revenue-run-rate-tops-65-billion-source-says-2026-08-17/',
  },
  {
    text: 'Yahoo Finance, S&P 500 (^GSPC) price history',
    href: 'https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EGSPC/history/',
  },
  {
    text: 'State Street Global Advisors, SPY daily holdings',
    href: 'https://www.ssga.com/library-content/products/fund-data/etfs/us/holdings-daily-us-en-spy.xlsx',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} - Rycode`}
  description={subhead}
  variant="economics"
>
  <article class="econ-digest econ-brief">
    <header class="econ-digest-head">
      <h1 class="econ-digest-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="econ-digest-subhead">{subhead}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="econ-digest-main">
      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="backstory">
        <h2>Backstory</h2>
        <p>In the late 1990s, internet companies were often valued on metrics that were not dollars. A study of internet stocks in 1998 and 1999 found that page views and unique visitors helped explain their prices, while net income did not. The crash in early 2000 wiped out long tails of companies. Every new bull market, that scar tissue asks the same question: is this a bubble again?</p>
        <p>This cycle has dollars where that scare cycle had vanity metrics. Nvidia, which sells the chips inside AI data centers, reported $96.2 billion of revenue and $59.7 billion of net income for the quarter ended 26 Jul 2026. Anthropic's revenue run rate, a yearly pace projected from current sales, topped $65 billion by the end of July, according to Reuters. Products are in market. Customers pay. Euphoria can still price a real market too high. The clock is when that repricing comes, not whether the revenue is real.</p>
        <p>On 9 Sep 2026 this page argued that late private rounds at 50 to 100 times revenue look like late dot-com checks: a proven operator can defend a high multiple, a high price for each dollar of revenue, and a thin wrapper cannot keep one forever. Delaying a raise to wait for a higher price risks a closed window.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="where-we-stand">
        <h2>Where we stand</h2>
        <p class="econ-brief-asof">{brief.asOf}</p>
        <p>{brief.market}</p>
        {brief.top10 && <p>{brief.top10}</p>}
        <p>{brief.strain}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="watching">
        <h2>What we're watching</h2>
        <RiskWatch items={watchItems} class="econ-watch" />
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section econ-digest-sources" id="sources">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          {sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <a href={source.href}>{source.text}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
